{"seq":1,"at":"2026-09-17","kind":"imported","target":{"type":"article","id":"a1101"},"reason":"Article of the Quiztopia bank (written for card c092): researched and transcribed in September 2026, converted to the quiz platform on 2026-10-20.","by":"Quiztopia import","ref":"quiz-archive/transcripts/quiztopia/c092.json"},
{"seq":2,"at":"2026-10-20","kind":"edited","target":{"type":"article","id":"a1101"},"reason":"Rewritten by the merge to take in the printed cards' questions.","by":"merge science/medicine","ref":"quiz-archive/runs/tp-ingest/decisions/science__medicine.json","changes":[{"field":"bodyEn","diff":[["=",794],["+","Why allergies have become so common in wealthy countries is still debated. The best-known explanation is the hygiene hypothesis, proposed in 1989 by the British epidemiologist David Strachan, who noticed that children with many older siblings suffered less often from hay fever. In its popular form it holds that children who play in dirt, or grow up on farms and among animals, are less susceptible to allergies and asthma than other children, because early contact with microorganisms teaches the immune system to tolerate harmless substances; researchers today prefer to speak of the \"old friends\" that a sterile modern childhood lacks.\n\n"],["=",311]]},{"field":"bodyDe","diff":[["=",730],["+","Warum Allergien in wohlhabenden Ländern so häufig geworden sind, ist bis heute umstritten. Die bekannteste Erklärung ist die Hygienehypothese, 1989 von dem britischen Epidemiologen David Strachan aufgestellt, dem aufgefallen war, dass Kinder mit vielen älteren Geschwistern seltener an Heuschnupfen litten. In ihrer populären Form besagt sie, dass Kinder, die im Schmutz spielen oder auf Bauernhöfen und mit Tieren aufwachsen, weniger anfällig für Allergien und Asthma sind als andere Kinder, weil der frühe Kontakt mit Mikroorganismen das Immunsystem lehrt, harmlose Stoffe zu dulden; Forscher sprechen heute lieber von den „alten Freunden“, die einer keimarmen modernen Kindheit fehlen.\n\n"],["=",277]]}]},
